Here's the summary of what was created:

### Files created

**`docker-compose.runpod.yml`** — Compose override for RunPod
- `celery-worker` service with GPU reservation, connecting to production DB/Redis via `host.docker.internal` (SSH tunnel)
- `finetune` one-shot service (activated with `--profile finetune`)
- Models volume bound to `/workspace/models` (RunPod persistent storage)

**`Dockerfile.runpod`** — GPU-enabled image based on `nvidia/cuda:12.4.1`
- Installs all ML extras (timesfm, autogluon, chronos)
- Includes scripts directory for fine-tuning

**`scripts/runpod-deploy.sh`** — One-command deployment script
- Sets up SSH tunnel from RunPod to production server
- Forwards DB (5432) and Redis (6379) ports
- Builds and starts the Celery worker
- Creates `/workspace/models` on persistent storage

### Usage on RunPod

```bash
# First time: clone repo to persistent storage
cd /workspace && git clone <repo-url> gorm.ai && cd gorm.ai

# Deploy (starts tunnel + worker)
PROD_HOST=your-server.com PROD_USER=root bash scripts/runpod-deploy.sh

# Fine-tune
docker compose -f docker-compose.runpod.yml run --rm finetune \
    --customer-ids <id> --start-date 2020-01-01

# View logs
docker compose -f docker-compose.runpod.yml logs -f
```

The key strength: to move to a new RunPod instance, just clone the repo to `/workspace`, set `PROD_HOST`, and run the deploy script. Model checkpoints survive on persistent storage.
