[2026-03-22T05:25:41Z] [CLAUDE] The `Dockerfile.runpod` already includes `--extra chronos` on line 27 — so `chronos-forecasting` **is** installed in the image. The problem is your RunPod worker is running an **older image** that was built before the `chronos` extra was added.

You need to **rebuild and redeploy** the RunPod image:

```bash
# 1. Build the updated image
docker build -f Dockerfile.runpod -t your-registry/gorm-ai-runpod:latest .

# 2. Push to your container registry
docker push your-registry/gorm-ai-runpod:latest

# 3. Restart/redeploy the RunPod pod (it will pull the new image)
```

The Dockerfile already installs all the engines you need:
- `--extra ml` → PyTorch, TimesFM, statsmodels, scikit-learn
- `--extra autogluon` → AutoGluon (Chronos 2 via AutoGluon wrapper, Toto)
- `--extra chronos` → `chronos-forecasting` (Chronos 2 direct engine)

After redeployment, you can verify it works by checking the worker logs for:
```
chronos-forecasting is available
Chronos model loaded: amazon/chronos-t5-base (pipeline: ChronosTPipeline)
```

instead of the current:
```
chronos-forecasting not installed; falling back to statistical
```

**Tip**: If you want to pre-download the model weights into the image (avoids download on first prediction), add this to the Dockerfile before the `USER appuser` line:

```dockerfile
RUN python -c "from chronos import BaseChronosPipeline; BaseChronosPipeline.from_pretrained('amazon/chronos-t5-base')" \
    && mv /root/.cache/huggingface /models/hf-cache
ENV HF_HUB_CACHE=/models/hf-cache
```
