[2026-03-23T09:35:10Z] [CLAUDE] Ah, so it did eventually get caught — but slowly. The detection took a while because of two compounding delays:

1. **Wrong worker name**: The RunPod worker registered as `"local"` (same as the local worker) because `WORKER_NAME` wasn't set in `docker-compose.runpod.yml`. This meant `mark_orphaned_worker_tasks_failed` couldn't detect it — it saw `worker_name="local"` which was in the alive set.

2. **Fallback path**: It only got marked failed when either `mark_stale_tasks_failed` ran (requires ALL workers to be down) or `mark_stale_pending_revoked` caught it after much longer.

I've added `WORKER_NAME=runpod-gpu` to `docker-compose.runpod.yml`. Next time a RunPod instance is terminated, the orphan detection will catch it within ~2 minutes (the ping interval) + 10 minutes (the staleness check), since `"runpod-gpu"` won't be in the alive workers set.

You'll need to redeploy the RunPod worker for this to take effect.
