[2026-03-24T06:35:40Z] [CLAUDE] All clean. Here's a summary of what was wrong and what I fixed:

**Root cause — two bugs:**

1. **`updated_at` never updates during progress callbacks.** `update_progress` uses a Core-level SQL `UPDATE`, but `updated_at` has `onupdate=utcnow` which only fires for ORM-level attribute changes. So `updated_at` stays frozen at task creation time, and the `stale_seconds=600` check in orphan detection always sees the task as stale.

2. **Redis worker registry expires during long tasks.** The registry key (2-hour TTL) is only refreshed on `task_prerun`/`task_postrun`. A task running > 2 hours causes the key to expire, so the ping endpoint can't see the worker as registered.

3. **Ping endpoint didn't check Redis registry.** It only checked Celery `inspect().ping()` (which solo-pool workers can't respond to while busy) and a Docker fallback (local only).

**Fixes:**

- **`task.py:update_progress`** — explicitly sets `updated_at=datetime.now(UTC)` so progress acts as a heartbeat
- **`celery_app.py`** — exported `refresh_worker_registry()` function
- **All three task files** (simulations, predictions, finetuning) — progress callbacks now call `refresh_worker_registry()` to keep the Redis TTL alive
- **`tasks.py:ping_workers`** — now checks the Redis worker registry as a fallback before declaring workers dead, covering all remote workers (not just local Docker)
- **Reset the incorrectly failed task** `d9b94134-ce91-494e-b1b3-c2e44e6223ff` back to `started`
[2026-03-24T06:36:54Z] [USER] And the SYNC_TARGET & SYNC_EVERY (export SYNC_TARGET=) defined on the RunPod will be superceded by parameters defined on the model configuration, such that I can run finetuning for two different models on the same RunPod server?
[2026-03-24T06:38:52Z] [USER] The task (a new ID now: f9fd125c-6465-4861-a59f-36064789aa12) went back to finished with the error: Worker lost (instance terminated)

Worker still running
[2026-03-24T06:41:25Z] [CLAUDE] **Yes** — now the per-engine DB config (`finetune_sync_target`, `finetune_sync_every`, `finetuned_model_path`) supersedes the env vars. So on the same RunPod you can fine-tune two models and each will sync to its own target:

| Setting | Moirai (from DB) | TimesFM (from DB) | Env fallback |
|---|---|---|---|
| Model path | `models/finetune/moirai` | `models/finetune/timesfm` | `$FINETUNED_MODEL_PATH` |
| Sync target | `rune@server:.../moirai/` | `rune@server:.../timesfm/` | `$SYNC_TARGET` |
| Sync every | 10 | 10 | `$SYNC_EVERY` |

The priority chain is: **engine DB config → env var → hardcoded default**. The env vars on RunPod become the fallback, not the authority. Set the per-engine values in the AI Models → Finetune → Sync Parameters column in the UI.

The Finetune tab now has 3 fields in the sync column: Model Destination Directory, Sync Every, and Sync Target.
