[2026-03-28T03:09:34Z] [USER] ultrathink
I have written this document.

Can you identify any errors, inaccuracies, improvements, suggestions, additions, etc.


-------

Introduction info on GORM - AI successor to Cassandra.

AIM
* A generalized prediction engine, requiring no fine-tuning (zero shot), which can be applied to almost any forecast task regardless of business type or industry.

* Quality of prediction to be as good as, or better, than Cassandra.

* Model Zoo. Some AI Time Series models may excel better at certain tasks than other tasks. The aim is to have a variety of models, and choose the best model for the job. Ideally hot choosing the model at the point of prediction.

* Option to fine-tune for industries for which we have large amounts of data. This would still not be on a customer level.

* A natural language super layer, which can be queried using normal language. Both for historic results and future predictions. “How many newspapers were sold at 7-11s in October 2025?” “How many cars of the brand Toyota Hilux will be sold in second half of 2026?” “What impact will Thanksgiving have on sales quantity in the greater Philly area in 2027?” etc.

TECHNOLOGY
Modern AI Time Series models are generally of a decoder-only architecture. The same as modern LLMs (ChatGPT, Grok, ..) It is more akin to patter matching than statistical forecasts. The historical data is broken into small bites, 1 or a few days each, and these bites are recombined across the temporal context, the same way the conversation with ChatGPT is broken into bits and recombined in attention layers. Such that if for instance, the word “tower” is in a conversation then this points to one place, but it is combined with “Eiffel” from another place in the conversation, then it can be redirected to point to another location.

The models are termed Foundation Models. Meaning, large models trained on a very large dataset (hundreds of billions or trillions of data points), that can be further after trained for specific purposes.

DATA
The context is the amount of historical data (dates). Typically capped at 512, 1,024, or 2048. Such that for each outlet up to this number of historic dates can be fed into the engine to create prediction on.

Outlets are collected in batches. Typically of 8,16, or 32. But can be any number. The higher the batch count, the faster the calculations, and the more memory is required. Batching is done automatically and has no impact on the predictions.

All weekdays are calculated together, but if a product only has production on a single weekday, it may be beneficial to run this weekday as an isolated case.

COVARIATES
Predictions can be improved by included relevant covariates (including irrelevant covariates, may introduce noise and be detrimental to the quality of predictions).

Currently, the following covariates are included:
* Weekday
* Cost
* Pads

But many others could be relevant: temperature, precipitation, etc.

PREDICTIONS
The models produce quantile predictions. That is, a number of quantiles (typically 9) and a prediction, which is the median of the quantiles, or the middle quantile.

Each quantile has a quantity estimate (float) and a probability. Such that, for the Prediction quantile there is the highest probability that exactly this quantity will be sold.

Or expressed in another way, for quantile 10 (Q10) there is a 90% probability that this quantity (or a quantity larger than this) will be sold. For Q20 there is a 80%  probability that this quantity (or a quantity larger than this) will be sold; for Q90 there is a 10%  probability that this quantity (or a quantity larger than this) will be sold.

The quantile distribution reflects the sales variation. The larger the distance is between two quantiles (in terms of quantity, the probability is always fixed) the larger the variation. For accounts with very small variation, all quantities may be almost identical.

The quantiles gives rise to the Prediction. The model’s best guess for a sale.

Taking the Prediction from the model, this is further adjusted (outside the AI model) to reach the best amount to deliver to the end customer.

First the variation is calculated. Variation is already accounted for in the quantile distribution, but most likely not the complete variation picture. So an added variation component is added. What is calculated is CV (Coefficient of variation). This is calculated per Outlet per weekday.

Next the Economical Optimal is estimated, by taking the cost to profit ratio into consideration. This is done by applying the Newsvendor critical fractile formula (“The newsvendor (or newsboy or single-period or salvageable) model is a mathematical model in operations management and applied economics used to determine optimal inventory levels. It is (typically) characterized by fixed prices and uncertain demand for a perishable product.”)

τ (tau) = (selling_price - production_cost) / selling_price

τ then gets adjusted upward on high-variability weekdays: τ = τ + cv × (1 − τ).

Then the quantile closest to τ from the Q10-Q90 distribution is picked to be Economical Optimal. The idea: if margins are high (cost is small relative to price), τ is close to 1.0, so it picks a higher quantile (e.g. Q80/Q90) — you'd rather overstock than miss sales. If margins are thin, τ is lower, so it picks a lower quantile.

(change to formula: instead of picking the closest quantile, it now linear interpolates between the quantile below and the quantile above, and sets Economical Optimal to this value – that is, EO no longer corresponds to a quantile).

If τ is greater than Q90, then Economic Optimal is extrapolated beyond Q90 to Q99 (where it is capped).

Finally, there is a weekday correction adjustment. Specifically for the TimesFM model, but perhaps also for other models, this model is trained on dataset which heavily favors a higher Monday-Friday and lower weekend sales pattern. If this does not correspond to the sales pattern for the customer being predicted for, the weekday draws can be evened out by applying a weekday correction adjustment.

To arrive at a final (whole number) quantity prediction, the adjusted prediction is updated by any constraints given on the outlet (fixed, minimum, maximum, add#/add%) and finally rounded up to a whole number.

FINE-TUNING
The Foundation Models gives an out-of-the-box prediction capability, called zero-shot prediction. If we have a large amount of data to one particular business sector, the foundation model can be further improved by after training. It is unknown, but doubtful, if fine-tuning with data from one business sector can improve predictions in another business sector, may well in fact be detrimental.

Fine-tuning is done by a standard Gradient Descend method.

- Cost (Error) function
- Calculate gradient
- Adjust parameters (weights) (by Learning Rate) in the direction of the steepest descent
- Repeat (# of Epocs)

SIMULATION EVALUATION
In order to evaluate the quality of the predictions in comparison with Cassandra, a simulation functionality has been implemented.

The results of the simulation is broken down into four groups:

Group 1: Where prediction < actual quantity and > actual sale =>
lower quantity, no missed sale =>
cost reduction

Group 2: Where prediction < actual quantity, but also < actual sale =>
lower quantity, some missed sale =>
cost reduction - profit reduction

Group 3: Where prediction > actual sale and actual return > 0 =>
higher quantity, no extra sale =>
cost increase

Group 4: Where prediction > actual sale and actual return = 0 =>
Potential extra sale.

Estimating the profit of potential extra sale is done by estimating the probability of a sale for each extra quantity separately. The probabilities comes from the quantiles, however the quantiles were calculated prior to the assessment. When doing the assessment, we have more precise knowledge, so the probabilities have to be adjusted with this in mind. The actual sale is now treated as a floor (P=1.0) and predictions for quantiles beyond that are given a probability according to their exponential decay relative to the new floor.

- survival(x) = 0.10 × exp(-λ × (x - Q90))
- λ = ln(2) / (Q90 - Q80), meaning survival probability halves every Q80→Q90 spread

TERMINOLOGY

Foundation Model
An AI model which has not been fine-tuned.

Fine-tuning
After training the foundation model with own data. This will adjust the parameters.

Parameters (Weights)
Neurons or synapse.

Context
Lookback history

Horizon
Forecast length

GPU
Graphic Processing Unit. Necessary for doing predictions and finetuning.

Huggingface
Open-source platform in the artificial intelligence and machine learning space.
Samples

Batch Size
Number of accounts to make predictions for in the same calculation loop. Higher = faster, but more peak memory usage. No impact on predictions

