[2026-04-01T05:40:36Z] [CLAUDE] That covers the basics but you're missing dependencies your project actually needs. Based on your `pyproject.toml` and the engines, you need:

```bash
vastai create instance <OFFER_ID> \
  --image vastai/pytorch \
  --disk 20 \
  --ssh --direct \
  --onstart-cmd "
    pip install --upgrade pip && \
    pip install -q \
      timesfm[torch] \
      huggingface_hub \
      sqlalchemy[asyncio] asyncpg \
      celery[redis] redis \
      pydantic pydantic-settings \
      numpy pandas \
      scikit-learn statsmodels \
      structlog psutil \
      anthropic && \
    echo '✅ Ready'
  "
```

**What was missing and why:**

- **`sqlalchemy[asyncio] asyncpg`** - your workers write predictions, tokens, and covariates directly to PostgreSQL
- **`celery[redis] redis`** - the worker *is* a Celery worker connecting to your Redis broker
- **`pydantic pydantic-settings`** - config loading, all schemas
- **`scikit-learn`** - Ridge regression for covariates
- **`structlog`** - logging (including the new LLM log)
- **`anthropic`** - if you want the investigate feature available remotely

**Better approach:** Instead of installing packages individually, clone your repo and use `uv`:

```bash
vastai create instance <OFFER_ID> \
  --image vastai/pytorch \
  --disk 20 \
  --ssh --direct \
  --onstart-cmd "
    pip install uv && \
    git clone <your-repo> /app && \
    cd /app && \
    uv sync --extra ml && \
    echo '✅ Ready'
  "
```

This installs exactly what your `pyproject.toml` specifies, including the `ml` extras (statsmodels, sklearn, torch, timesfm, transformers). No version drift between local and remote.
