[2026-04-03T02:57:00Z] [USER] What is the difference between bfl16 and fl16?
[2026-04-03T02:57:15Z] [CLAUDE] Both are 16-bit floating point formats but with different precision/range tradeoffs:

| | **float16 (fp16)** | **bfloat16 (bf16)** |
|---|---|---|
| **Total bits** | 16 | 16 |
| **Exponent bits** | 5 | 8 |
| **Mantissa bits** | 10 | 7 |
| **Range** | ±65,504 | Same as float32 (~±3.4×10³⁸) |
| **Precision** | Higher | Lower |

**Key difference**: bfloat16 has the same exponent range as float32 (8 bits), so it rarely overflows/underflows during training. float16 has more precision per number but a much narrower range, making it prone to overflow in gradients.

**In practice for inference** (your use case): they perform nearly identically. Most modern models and GPUs support both. bfloat16 is generally the safer default — it "just works" without the scaling tricks that fp16 sometimes needs.
