[2026-04-03T07:54:35Z] [USER] In the context of native vers external covariate calculations. Thanksgiving do have very unusal demand
[2026-04-03T07:54:46Z] [CLAUDE] You're right — I was wrong to dismiss it. Thanksgiving itself, with its sharp demand drop, is exactly the kind of signal where native vs external should diverge, even with just financial covariates.

The key difference on a day like Thanksgiving:

- **External (Ridge):** Fits a linear model on residuals. If the base TimesFM forecast already misses the demand drop, Ridge can only adjust based on whatever covariate values happen to be present that day. With just `cost_per_unit`, there's nothing in the covariate signal that says "demand will crash today," so the adjustment is minimal.

- **Native (TimesFM):** The covariates are fed into the transformer alongside the time series. Even if the covariate values are similar to other days, the model's attention mechanism sees the full context — the historical pattern of demand drops at this point in the year combined with the covariate values. It can potentially learn the interaction between the time position and covariates.

So on Thanksgiving, the base forecast quality matters most. If TimesFM's base forecast already captures the seasonal holiday pattern from history, both modes may be similar. But if it doesn't, native mode has a better chance of picking up the signal because it processes covariates jointly with the time series, while external Ridge can only do a post-hoc linear correction.

The real test would be to run an exploration covering a period that includes Thanksgiving and compare the results.
