[2026-04-03T11:25:29Z] [USER] If I run a prediction of for instance 1000 outlets, will it divide the outlets so one GPU takes around 500 and the other one the other half?
[2026-04-03T11:25:44Z] [CLAUDE] No. The current implementation (including my changes) assigns one task = one worker = one GPU. A 1000-outlet prediction runs entirely on whichever GPU the worker is pinned to, processing outlets in sequential batches (e.g., 32 at a time on TimesFM).

The multi-GPU benefit today is only for **concurrent tasks** — if two customers each submit a prediction, they run on different GPUs simultaneously.

To split a single 1000-outlet task across GPUs, we'd need to fan out sub-tasks at the service layer. Want me to look into that? It would mean something like:

1. API receives 1000-outlet prediction request
2. Service splits outlets into N chunks (one per available GPU worker)
3. Dispatches N Celery sub-tasks, each routed to a different worker/GPU
4. Aggregates results when all sub-tasks complete

This would give ~linear speedup for large single predictions, but adds complexity around task coordination, progress tracking, and error handling.
