[2026-04-04T04:28:48Z] [CLAUDE] Pushed `d107d52`. All 17 start scripts now always install/upgrade uv before running `uv sync`, so the remote server will pick up a version that supports the new `pyproject.toml` features.
[2026-04-04T04:29:49Z] [USER] Is it being pushed to git. I keep getting: Already up to date.
[2026-04-04T04:29:59Z] [CLAUDE] That's `git pull` saying "Already up to date" — it means the remote already has the latest commits. That's expected and correct.

The `uv` parse error you're seeing is from the **old uv binary** that was already cached on the vast.ai machine. The `git pull` succeeds (the new code is there), but the old uv runs before the upgrade line takes effect.

The problem is ordering in the script:

```bash
git pull                    # ✅ gets new pyproject.toml
command -v uv ...           # ❌ OLD uv found, skips install
uv sync ...                 # ❌ old uv can't parse new pyproject.toml
```

With the fix we just pushed, the next run should work because the old `command -v uv` guard is gone — it now always upgrades uv before `uv sync`.

**Just run the start script again.** The `git pull` will say "Already up to date" (that's fine — the fix is already pulled), but this time it'll upgrade uv before syncing.
