[2026-04-06T09:52:33Z] [CLAUDE] Good catch. PAD features are all zeros in the future period (no events), so `coefficient * 0 = 0` — they shouldn't directly affect the forecast. But they do, indirectly:

**The Ridge regression intercept shifts when features are added/removed.** Ridge fits `intercept + coefficients * features` on historical residuals. When PAD features are included, they explain some historical variance (dates that DID have PAD events in the training window), which changes the intercept and the coefficients of all other features. The intercept applies to every future day regardless of PAD values.

In short: including PAD features distorts the Ridge regression fit even though the future PAD values are zero. The model is "wasting" degrees of freedom on historical PAD patterns that don't apply to this 14-day window.

This is consistent with "None" being the best result — for a short 14-day window with no special events, the Ridge regression adjustment is doing more harm than good.

One potential improvement: skip PAD features in `_build_covariate_arrays` when there are no PAD event dates in the future prediction window (not just when the covariate type is disabled). Want me to implement that?
[2026-04-06T09:57:13Z] [USER] yes
[2026-04-06T09:58:55Z] [CLAUDE] Pushed `0403403`. All 9 engines now check `future_set & event_dates` before including a PAD feature — if no PAD events fall in the forecast window, that PAD is excluded entirely from Ridge regression, preventing intercept distortion.
