[2026-04-08T01:53:40Z] [CLAUDE] I can't reliably enumerate the current vast.ai template catalog — it's community-curated and changes constantly, and the templates page requires login so I can't fetch it directly. But here's what I know to be true about which Docker images typically ship flash-attn pre-installed; you can search the vast.ai template list for any of these base images:

**Images that ship flash-attn:**
- **`nvcr.io/nvidia/pytorch:24.xx-py3`** (NVIDIA NGC PyTorch) — versions from 23.10+ include flash-attn 2.x prebuilt against the matching torch/CUDA. Probably your best bet for Blackwell since NGC ships cu128 quickly.
- **`vllm/vllm-openai`** — vLLM's official image has flash-attn baked in (it's a hard runtime dep)
- **`ghcr.io/huggingface/text-generation-inference`** — TGI image, flash-attn is required
- **`winglian/axolotl`** — finetuning image, flash-attn pre-installed
- **`runpod/pytorch:2.x.x-py3.xx-cuda12.x.x-devel-ubuntu22.04`** — some variants include it; varies by tag

**Images that do NOT ship flash-attn (will hit your current source-build path):**
- `pytorch/pytorch:*` (official PyTorch image — bare torch only)
- Most "vast.ai PyTorch" generic templates
- Plain Ubuntu + CUDA images

**How to check before launching a vast.ai instance:**

1. On the templates page, look for templates whose base image starts with `nvcr.io/nvidia/pytorch`, `vllm/`, `ghcr.io/huggingface/`, or `winglian/axolotl`
2. After picking one, before running your start script, SSH in and run: `python -c "import flash_attn; print(flash_attn.__version__)"` — if it imports, you're good and you can short-circuit the build step in `start-yinglong.sh`
3. If flash-attn IS present but for a different torch version, you'll need to be careful: the force-reinstall of torch nightly cu128 in your script will likely break the ABI link to the prebuilt flash-attn. In that case, either skip the torch reinstall or accept that you'll still need a source build.

**A practical alternative if you want to stop fighting this**: NVIDIA's NGC PyTorch container `nvcr.io/nvidia/pytorch:25.01-py3` (or whatever the latest is when you read this) is purpose-built around Blackwell, includes torch+cu128+flash-attn already matched, and is available as a vast.ai template (search "ngc pytorch"). Using it would let you drop both the torch nightly reinstall AND the flash-attn source build from your script.

If you want, I can add a guard to `start-yinglong.sh` that detects an existing working `flash_attn` install and skips the build entirely — that way the same script works on both flash-attn-preinstalled and bare images. Worth doing?
