[2026-04-08T05:04:53Z] [USER] The server crashed. Now up and running again. But now I get these error messages on the vast server: 2026-04-08T05:04:21.483217Z [error    ] consumer: Cannot connect to redis://localhost:6379/1: Error 111 connecting to localhost:6379. Connection refused..
Trying again in 16.00 seconds... (8/100)
[2026-04-08T05:05:23Z] [CLAUDE] The vast worker reaches Redis/Postgres through an SSH tunnel back to `gorm.predictioninstitute.com` (`-L 6379:localhost:6379`, `-L 5432:localhost:5433`). When the main server crashed, the `ssh -f -N` tunnel process on the vast box died and was never restarted — so `redis://localhost:6379/1` on vast now points at nothing, hence "Connection refused".

The worker process itself is still running and will keep retrying forever; it won't repair the tunnel on its own.

Fix: re-establish the tunnel on the vast box. Easiest is to re-run the start script there (it kills any stale tunnel first and recreates it), e.g. on the vast instance:

```bash
bash ~/workspace/projects/gorm.ai/scripts/vast.ai/start-timesfm.sh
```

(or whichever start-*.sh matches that worker — `start-group-*.sh` if it's a group worker). That will:
1. `ssh` to gorm and ensure `docker compose up -d redis` is running,
2. recreate the `-L 5432 / -L 6379` SSH tunnel,
3. wait until port 6379 is reachable.

Once the tunnel is back, the existing Celery worker's retry loop should reconnect on its next attempt — no need to restart it. If you'd rather not re-run the whole script, just the `ssh -f -N -L ...` block from lines 11–18 is enough to restore connectivity.

Worth checking longer term: nothing is supervising that tunnel. A small autossh wrapper (or systemd unit with `Restart=always`) on the vast boxes would prevent this from recurring next time the main host blips.
[2026-04-08T05:06:55Z] [USER] Can you add a such an autossh wrapper. I was using the scripts under: scripts/vast.ai
[2026-04-08T05:18:24Z] [CLAUDE] All 12 worker scripts now source the helper, and the old `ssh -f -N` blocks are gone.

**What I added** — `scripts/vast.ai/_tunnel.sh`:
- Installs `autossh` if missing (apt-get, with sudo only if not root — fits vast root containers).
- Kills any stale autossh/ssh tunnel for the same forwards before starting (safe to re-run).
- Ensures `redis` is up on the main host (preserves existing behavior).
- Starts `autossh -M 0 -f -N` with the same `-L 5432:localhost:5433` and `-L 6379:localhost:6379` forwards, plus the original `ServerAliveInterval=30 / CountMax=3 / ExitOnForwardFailure=yes` keepalives. `-M 0` disables autossh's monitoring port and relies on ssh keepalives instead — the modern recommended setup.
- `AUTOSSH_GATETIME=0` so it retries immediately even if the very first connection fails (handles "main server still booting" case).
- Logs to `~/.gorm-tunnel/autossh.log` on the vast box.
- Waits up to 20s for local 6379 to become reachable before returning.

**What changed in each `start-*.sh`** — the 26-line tunnel block at the top is replaced with:
```bash
# Self-healing autossh tunnel to the main gorm host (forwards 5432 + 6379)
source "$(dirname "$0")/_tunnel.sh"
```

**To deploy on a vast box**, just `git pull` and re-run the relevant `start-*.sh` once. From then on, if the main host crashes or the network blips, autossh will reconnect automatically and the Celery worker's own retry loop will pick the broker back up — no manual intervention needed.

One thing to be aware of: `_tunnel.sh` uses `set -euo pipefail`, and because the worker scripts `source` it, those flags will persist into the rest of the script. The existing scripts already set the same flags on line 2, so this is a no-op — but worth knowing if you ever add a script that doesn't.
