[2026-04-09T06:24:56Z] [CLAUDE] Confirmed. **Yes, this is a bug.** The simulation code does NOT honor the fine-tuned model strategy.

## The bug

In `src/gorm_ai/services/prediction.py:71-110`, single predictions extract `finetuned_model` and `finetuned_model_base_path` from the strategy and, when set, **directly instantiate `TimesFMFinetunedEngine` with the checkpoint path**:

```python
if finetuned_model and finetuned_model_base_path:
    checkpoint_path = os.path.join(finetuned_model_base_path, finetuned_model)
    engine = TimesFMFinetunedEngine(checkpoint_path=checkpoint_path, ...)
else:
    engine = self.engine_registry.get_engine(engine_type)
```

In `src/gorm_ai/services/simulation.py:267-282`, the simulation copies the strategy-loading boilerplate but **omits the entire finetuned_model branch**. It only pulls `strategy_engine_slug` and then calls `engine_registry.get_engine(engine_type)` unconditionally — so it always gets the base `timesfm` engine, never `TimesFMFinetunedEngine`, regardless of what the strategy says.

That's why your two simulations (`4e6a8ec9…` and `d331c6bc…`) produced identical results — both ran the base TimesFM model.

## Why single predictions worked

`prediction.py:99-106` is the only place that constructs `TimesFMFinetunedEngine` with a checkpoint. The registry lookup path (used by simulation) cannot reach it, because the registered `TIMESFM_FINETUNED` entry has no way to know *which* checkpoint to load — that information lives on the strategy and only `prediction.py` reads it.

## Fix

Lift the finetuned-model resolution out of `prediction.py:71-110` and apply it in `simulation.py:267-282` too. Concretely, simulation needs to:

1. Read `strategy.finetuned_model` and `strategy.prediction_engine.finetuned_model_path` (alongside the existing `strategy_engine_slug` extraction at line 277).
2. If both are set, instantiate `TimesFMFinetunedEngine(checkpoint_path=..., allow_fallback=...)` instead of calling `engine_registry.get_engine(engine_type)` at line 282.

The cleanest version would be to factor that logic into a helper on `PredictionService` (e.g. `_resolve_engine_instance(strategy, engine_type, allow_fallback)`) and call it from both places, so they can't drift again.

Want me to apply the fix? If yes, I'd recommend the helper-method approach so we don't have two copies of the conditional.
[2026-04-09T06:28:52Z] [USER] yes, fix it. Use your recommeded solution
[2026-04-09T06:29:55Z] [CLAUDE] Done. Summary of fix:

1. **`src/gorm_ai/services/prediction.py`** — extracted the finetuned-vs-base engine selection into a new helper `PredictionService._resolve_engine_instance(engine_type, finetuned_model, finetuned_model_base_path, allow_fallback)`. Single-prediction code path now calls it instead of inlining the conditional.

2. **`src/gorm_ai/services/simulation.py`** — now reads `strategy.finetuned_model` and `strategy.prediction_engine.finetuned_model_path` (which it previously ignored), then calls the same `_resolve_engine_instance` helper. Both code paths share one source of truth.

3. Restarted `celery-worker` and `app` containers to pick up the source change.

