[2026-05-14T07:39:14Z] [CLAUDE] Yes — that's an important gap. Right now "Why now" only argues *timing* (foundation models exist), not *why they're better than what they replace*. An investor will absolutely ask this. The strongest version expands "Why now" into a combined **why now + why AI** section so both arguments live together. Suggested replacement:

---

## Why now / Why AI

Foundation models for time-series went production-ready in 2024–2026 (TimesFM 2.5, Chronos 2, Moirai 2). They are categorically different from the statistical methods (ARIMA, ETS, Prophet) that power incumbent forecasting tools — and better on the dimensions that matter to operators:

- **Zero-shot scale.** A statistical model has to be fit and re-fit to *each* individual series — painful when a retailer has 100,000 SKU-outlet combinations. Foundation models forecast any new series instantly, lifted by patterns learned across millions of others. Time-to-first-forecast for a new customer drops from weeks to minutes.
- **Native uncertainty.** Foundation models output a full demand distribution (P10–P90 quantiles), not a single number. This is exactly what the Newsvendor / Economic Optimal layer needs to convert a forecast into a profit-maximising decision — and what classical methods can't natively provide.
- **Robust on noisy, sparse, holiday-heavy data.** Statistical methods break or need hand-tuning when history is short or irregular. Foundation models degrade gracefully because they're leaning on priors learned elsewhere, not just on your series.
- **Improves with every model release.** TimesFM 3, Chronos 3, Moirai 3 will lift every GORM customer without us retraining anything. Classical statistics is decades-mature; the upside is gone.

What used to be a 12-month custom-model build is now a model-orchestration problem. **First mover in the orchestration + decisioning layer wins**, the same way Vercel won the hosting layer above Next.js.

---

Two things worth flagging:

1. **The native-quantile bullet is the most important one for *your* story** — it's the bridge that makes the Newsvendor profit layer work cleanly. Without quantiles you'd have to bootstrap a distribution yourself, which is exactly the kind of fragility that breaks statistical pipelines. This is the technical reason the AI choice and the profit-optimisation choice reinforce each other.
2. **You may want to soft-pedal the "classical statistics is decades-mature" line** if any pilot customers run hybrid Prophet/ARIMA today — it can read as dismissive of their existing setup. Happy to soften it (e.g., "complementary to, but no longer the frontier of, forecasting research") if that's a concern.
