[2026-05-14T09:41:42Z] [USER] To come back to this:

------------------

Phase 1 — purely deterministic, ships in a day or two. Create one OutletGroup per route, then write a small "route delivery audit" service:

1. For each outlet, compute baseline delivered per (weekday, month) using a robust median over the trailing N weeks (outlet_delivery already tells you which weekdays the outlet is
open).
2. Flag any open-weekday observation where delivered ≤ 20% of baseline as a skip candidate.
3. Pivot to (date, route) and surface dates where ≥k outlets in the same route fire on the same day → "likely driver skip event."
4. Per route, rank outlets by skip frequency and by correlation of their skips with other route members' skips (a single chronically-failing outlet looks different from a driver
bailing on a section).
5. If you populate sequence numbers in outlet_info (key like route_seq), add a final check: are the skipped outlets clustered at the end of the sequence? That's the strongest "driver
ran out of time" signal.

Phase 2 — AI layer on top. Reuse the /outlier_investigate pattern: feed the per-route findings (skip dates, affected outlets, sequence positions, weekday clustering) to Claude and
have it write a route-level narrative — "Route 14 has 7 skip events in the last 90 days, all Saturdays, all in the last 4 stops of the sequence; consistent with the driver shortening
the route." This is cheap given the infra you already have for chat/investigate.

Main tradeoff

Route detection works best when you can label the data — without per-outlet route assignments (and ideally sequence within route), you can still cluster outlets by geographic
proximity and infer routes, but the false-positive rate on "which outlets are on the same driver's run" goes up materially. If you have the route assignments in the source system,
importing them into OutletGroup is the single highest-leverage step; everything else is a few hundred lines on top.

------

