[2026-05-15T10:08:24Z] [USER] Can you ammend the pitch so it is more directed towards potential customers. Not sure this will change the pitch, but since it is an application under development, to start with we're mostly looking for customers willing to be part of this for only a synbolic amount.
[2026-05-15T10:09:28Z] [CLAUDE] This is a real rewrite — customer pitches and investor pitches answer different questions. An investor wants TAM, moat, team. A customer wants "what does this do for me, does it work, what does it cost, what's the risk if I sign up?" The testimonials and product story carry over; the rest gets reframed.

Here's a first draft pitched at early-adopter / design-partner customers:

---

# GORM — Forecasting that already saves our customers seven figures a year

**Prediction Institute · Early-access programme · 2026-05**

> "Cassandra has provided us with an annual saving of more than €1 million without impacting the sale."
> — **Torben Pilgård Madsen**, Market Manager, *Ekstra Bladet*

## What GORM does for you

GORM tells you **how many of each product to send to each location, every day**, in a way that maximises your profit — not just forecast accuracy. It learns from your sales history, weather, holidays, promotions and prices, and turns that into an actual delivery quantity per outlet, per day. Customers who run our existing engine, *Cassandra*, see:

- **Lower waste** — fewer copies/units returned unsold.
- **Fewer stock-outs** — high-margin locations get more, automatically.
- **Less manual work** — no spreadsheets, no per-SKU tuning, no in-house data team required.

## Who we are

Prediction Institute has built and operated **Cassandra**, the forecasting system behind *The Washington Post, The New York Times, Chicago Sun-Times, Philadelphia Inquirer, Denver Post, Toronto Star* and *Ekstra Bladet* — among 30 newsroom customers across the US, Canada and Europe — for over a decade. **GORM is Cassandra's next generation**: the same domain DNA, rebuilt on top of the new wave of AI forecasting models.

> "The Prediction Institute team did an exceptional job working with our internal IT team … The only regret we have is that we did not move to the system earlier."
> — **Jim Dean**, Director, Delivery Operations & Administration, *The Washington Post*

## Why GORM, and why now

Until 2024, accurate forecasting at outlet × product × day level required a custom statistical model, fitted and re-fitted by a specialist data team. In the last two years, a new generation of AI forecasting models (Google TimesFM, Amazon Chronos, Salesforce Moirai and others) has changed that. GORM blends 20+ of these models into a single ensemble and adds the layer that matters: **cost, margin and price are folded directly into the answer**, so the output is the *profit-maximising* delivery quantity, not an abstract demand number.

In practice that means:

- **Works on your data out of the box** — no per-series fitting, no PhD team.
- **Handles the messy stuff well** — short history, irregular holidays, promotions, weather-driven swings.
- **Gets better automatically** — every new model release lifts every customer; no upgrade project required.
- **Ask in plain English** — *"Sunday circulation, Chicago metro, Q4"* returns a forecast, scenario or chart.

## The early-access programme

GORM is in beta and we are inviting a small group of customers to join the programme. In return for a **symbolic licence fee for the duration of the pilot**, you get:

- Full platform access and a fine-tuned model trained on your own historical data.
- Hands-on onboarding and a direct line to the founders.
- Custom holiday, event and promotion handling built around your operation.
- Pricing locked in at design-partner rates when the programme ends.

What we ask in return: your historical sales data, periodic feedback, and — if results match what we've seen with Cassandra customers — permission to reference you publicly.

We are looking for businesses with **multiple delivery locations, daily SKU-level sales history, and meaningful per-unit margins** — newspapers, magazines, perishable retail, route-delivered goods. Programme is capped at a small number of partners.

## Talk to us

Email **`[contact@predictioninstitute.com]`** with a one-line description of your operation. We'll come back within 48 hours with whether GORM is a fit and what an early-access engagement would look like.

---

A few things worth deciding on the next pass:

1. **Which vertical to lead with.** I leaned on the newspaper proof points because that's where the testimonials, the customer list and the €1M number live — and credibility is everything for a customer ask. If the design-partner programme is specifically aimed at *non-newspaper* prospects (retail, hospitality, etc.), the proof-points still apply but the eligibility line and tone should shift. Easy to do a second version.
2. **"Symbolic fee" wording.** I left it abstract. Some founders prefer a concrete anchor ("EUR 1 / month", "EUR 500 / month covering compute") because it sets the perception of value better than "symbolic" — which can read as "free, and therefore worthless" to a buyer. Worth deciding.
3. **What counts as "results"** for the public-reference clause. Most design-partner agreements name a specific metric threshold (e.g. ">5% profit lift in a 90-day measurement window") — sharper than "if results match what we've seen."
4. **Contact email.** I put a placeholder; tell me the address you want.
[2026-05-15T10:24:29Z] [USER] Would it be more useful / common business practise to have it fill two pages instead of one?
[2026-05-15T10:24:56Z] [CLAUDE] For a customer-facing early-access / design-partner brief, **two pages is actually the more common format** — and probably the more useful one here. Here's the rule of thumb:

- **One page** is right for *top-of-funnel* material: cold outreach attachments, trade-show leave-behinds, anything a stranger reads in 90 seconds before deciding whether to take a meeting. Anything longer doesn't get read cold.
- **Two pages** is right for *post-first-conversation* material: the document you send after a discovery call when someone is genuinely considering joining. They want to share data, sign even a symbolic agreement, and commit time — they need more than 90 seconds of context to say yes.

For a design-partner programme specifically, two pages is close to standard. Page one stays as it is (value, proof, what GORM does, why now). Page two would cover the things customers reasonably want to know before committing — most of which I currently glossed over:

- **What the pilot actually looks like** — timeline, milestones, who does what.
- **What we need from you** — concrete data list (sales history depth, outlet/SKU master, costs/prices, calendar).
- **What you get back** — deliverables, integration touchpoints, dashboards, reports.
- **A short case study or two with real numbers** (Ekstra Bladet €1M, expand into the operational story).
- **FAQ** — data security, IP ownership of the fine-tuned model, exit terms, what happens at end of pilot, integration with your existing ERP/route-management.
- **Pricing logic and timeline** — symbolic during pilot, design-partner rate after, lock-in length.

My recommendation: **keep the one-pager as the cold-outreach version, and let me draft a two-page version for serious prospects.** Most B2B early-access programmes I've seen do exactly this — a one-page teaser and a two-page brief, same brand, used at different stages of the funnel.

Want me to extend it to the two-page version?
[2026-05-17T12:33:37Z] [USER] When I look at a single outlet under route 308
Outlet: ID= e47cc9aa-baa1-4f22-a207-a4d2b8697024
ext-id= 146
I get three zero sales dates for the period after 2026.01.01

Date         ext-id     delivered sold
2026-04-14    146    4    0
2026-02-07    146    4    0
2026-01-31    146    3    0

But from the analyse model I see two hits (1 Skips, 1 Phantom - not sure this counts as 1 or 2)
See attached image.[Image #2]
[2026-05-17T12:36:50Z] [CLAUDE] Mystery fully diagnosed. Three things are happening:

**1. "1 Skip / 1 Phantom" is one event, not two.** Phantom is a subset of Skip — the row says "this outlet has 1 skip day, and 1 of those days had `delivered > 0`". Not two separate events.

**2. The 1 skip the audit found isn't any of your three dates** — it's **2025-12-19 (Fri)** with `sold=0, delivered=5, baseline=3`. That fell inside your audit window, passed every filter, and triggered. The phantom count = 1 because `delivered=5`.

**3. Each of your three dates was filtered for a different reason:**

| Date | Weekday | Filter reason |
|---|---|---|
| 2026-01-31 | Sat | Inside an active **sales-filter** range (Sat baseline is also only 2, which would have failed `min_baseline=3` anyway) |
| 2026-02-07 | Sat | Same — inside the sales-filter range |
| 2026-04-14 | Tue | Tuesday baseline is only **2 papers** (median of non-zero Tuesday sales in the window). Below the `min_baseline=3` floor, so the day is dropped — the detector treats a zero-sale on a "normally sells 2" weekday as too noisy to call. |

Per-weekday baselines for this outlet in the window were Mon=2, Tue=2, Wed=3, Thu=2, Fri=3, Sat=2 — so only Wed and Fri pass the floor; everything else gets skipped (in the detection sense, not the delivery sense).

## The bigger picture

`min_baseline=3` is a deliberately conservative floor. It exists because if an outlet's typical sold is, say, 1–2 papers, a zero-sale day carries almost no information — it could be a slow Tuesday. By requiring "normally sells ≥ 3 on this weekday", we get statistical signal back.

But the side effect is exactly what you're seeing: low-volume outlets get many of their zero-sale days filtered out. The earlier "371 of 684 outlets excluded" note for Teak Penn was telling you that more than half this customer's outlets are below the floor on every weekday.

## Options

Three things you could do, in increasing order of effort:

1. **Lower `min_baseline` to 2** for this customer — a one-line default change, or pass it in the audit request. The trade-off is more false-positive flags from outlets that just have a slow day. Worth trying to see if the precision/recall balance is better for low-volume publications like Teak Penn.
2. **Expose `min_baseline` and `skip_threshold_pct` as UI controls** in the Cohorts tab, so each user can tune for their domain. Probably the right answer if different customers want different floors.
3. **Make the floor adaptive** — e.g., use the outlet's typical day-to-day variability rather than a fixed integer. More principled but more work.

