## Where the whole investigation landed

You started with "can the models forecast crypto, and is direction more realistic than price?" — and we now have a clean, multiply-validated answer:

- **Direction is a dead end** — proven three independent ways (model-free random-walk battery, TimesFM at 46.7%, and a momentum backtest negative on all 15 coins across the size spectrum). Your instinct to predict direction instead of price was the right *reframe*, but the data simply has no exploitable directional signal at these horizons. Finetuning can't fix that — there's nothing to learn.
- **Volatility is the real target** — and TimesFM, zero-shot, beats fitted GARCH(1,1) (corr 0.50 vs 0.29, −19% MAE, 71% regime accuracy), because it captures the daily vol seasonality GARCH can't. This is where your finetune pipeline and infrastructure would actually pay off.
- **Monetization** runs through volatility: most accessibly as risk/position-sizing (needs only a good forecast — you have one), and at the hard end as options trading (must beat *implied* vol — untested, higher bar).

**Artifacts** (all in `~/Downloads/btc/`): `fetch_binance.sh`, `null_hypothesis_test.py`, `multi_coin_screen.py`, `timesfm_walkforward.py`, `volatility_walkforward_v2.py`, plus the isolated `tfm-venv` and the data.

The single highest-value next step, if you continue: **seasonal GARCH / HAR-RV as the tougher benchmark** — if TimesFM still beats a model that explicitly includes the daily seasonality, the vol-forecasting edge is solid enough to build a real feature on. Happy to set that up whenever.
